<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Every major flight search engine defaults to showing all results — nonstop and connecting flights mixed together, usually sorted by price. The cheapest result is almost always a connection. If you want to see only direct flights, you need to know where each platform hides its filter. It is never in the same place twice.</p>
      <p>This guide walks you through the exact steps on the four most popular search engines. At the end we cover what to do when your route has no nonstop option at all, and when accepting a connection is the right call.</p>
      <h2>How to Filter for Nonstop on Skyscanner</h2>
      <p>Skyscanner is one of the most widely used flight search tools worldwide. Its nonstop filter is accessible once you know where to look.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Enter your origin, destination, and travel dates on the Skyscanner homepage and click Search.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> On the results page, look for the Stops filter. On desktop it appears in the left sidebar. On mobile it sits behind the filter icon at the top of the screen.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Select Direct only and the results will immediately update to show only nonstop flights.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Sort by price to find the cheapest nonstop available. Note that Skyscanner sometimes includes codeshare flights under slightly different airline names — always confirm the operating carrier before booking.</p>
      <div class="tip-box"><p>💡 Skyscanner's Everywhere search lets you search from your city to all destinations simultaneously with the direct-only filter applied. Useful if you are flexible on destination and want to find the cheapest nonstop flight from your airport regardless of where it goes.</p></div>
      <h2>How to Filter for Nonstop on Google Flights</h2>
      <p>Google Flights is arguably the most powerful consumer flight search tool available, and its nonstop filter is the easiest to use of any major platform.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Go to flights.google.com and enter your route and dates.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> On the results page, find the Stops dropdown in the top filter bar. It is typically the third or fourth option from the left.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Click it and select Nonstop only. Results update instantly.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Use Google Flights' calendar view to see prices across a full month — the fastest way to find the cheapest day for a nonstop flight on your route.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 5:</strong> Set a price alert. Google Flights tracks specific routes and emails you when the nonstop fare changes. This is one of the best free fare monitoring tools available anywhere.</p>
      <div class="info-box"><p>ℹ️ Google Flights pulls data from most major airlines but does not always include budget carriers like Spirit, Frontier, or many international low-cost carriers. Always check those airlines' websites separately after running your Google Flights search.</p></div>
      <h2>How to Filter for Nonstop on Kayak</h2>
      <p>Kayak has historically been strong for power users who want to filter and slice data across multiple dimensions at once.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Enter your route and dates on kayak.com and hit Search.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> On the results page, find the Stops filter in the left sidebar. Click Nonstop to filter to direct flights only.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Kayak's Hacker Fares feature sometimes combines a nonstop outbound with a nonstop return from a different airline at a lower combined price than booking round-trip on one carrier. Worth checking when price is the priority.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Use Kayak's Explore feature to browse nonstop options from your home airport on a price map. Similar to Skyscanner Everywhere but with more granular filtering options.</p>
      <h2>How to Filter for Nonstop on Expedia</h2>
      <p>Expedia is a full travel booking platform rather than a pure flight search engine, so its interface is more cluttered — but the nonstop filter is available.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Enter your route and dates on expedia.com and search flights.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> On the results page, look for the Stops section in the left-hand filter panel. Check the Nonstop box.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Expedia sometimes shows package deals (flight plus hotel) that undercut standalone flight prices significantly. If you need accommodation at your destination, compare the package price against booking separately before committing.</p>
      <div class="tip-box"><p>💡 Expedia, Kayak, and Skyscanner all pull from overlapping but not identical databases. If you find a good nonstop fare on one, verify on the airline's own website before booking — you will often save the booking fee and have an easier time with changes or refunds when booked direct.</p></div>
      <h2>What to Do When No Nonstop Exists</h2>
      <p>Some routes simply do not have nonstop service. Here is how to think through your options when no direct flight exists.</p>
      <h3>Check nearby airports</h3>
      <p>If your origin airport has no nonstop to your destination, the nearest major hub might. A two-hour drive to a larger departure airport is often worth it for a nonstop flight, particularly on long-haul routes where a bad connection can add eight or more hours to your journey. For many travelers in the UK or Canada, getting to London Heathrow or Toronto Pearson is almost always the answer — these airports have nonstop service to far more destinations than any regional airport.</p>
      <h3>Assess the connection risk honestly</h3>
      <p>Not all connections are equal. A three-hour layover in a reliable hub like Singapore Changi, Munich, or Zurich is a very different proposition from a 75-minute connection at Chicago O'Hare in January. Use tools like FlightAware to check historical on-time rates for the specific flight pairings you are considering before booking.</p>
      <h3>Look at seasonal nonstop service</h3>
      <p>Some routes that appear to have no nonstop option actually have seasonal direct service in summer or over winter holidays. Always check a full year's worth of dates before concluding a route has no nonstop. Airlines add and remove routes seasonally, and what is unavailable in February might be a daily nonstop in July.</p>
      <h3>Consider the layover length carefully</h3>
      <p>Short layovers under 90 minutes for domestic or under two hours for international carry real risk of missed connections. Layovers of four or more hours are safe but exhausting. The sweet spot for most connections is two to three hours — enough buffer to clear immigration and re-clear security if needed, but not so long that you are stranded for half a day.</p>
      <div class="info-box"><p>ℹ️ Some airlines operate technical stops where the plane lands to refuel but passengers stay on board and do not clear customs. This is technically not a nonstop flight but is far less disruptive than a genuine layover connection. Check the booking details carefully to understand what you are buying.</p></div>
      <h2>The Best Strategy: Alerts on Multiple Platforms</h2>
      <p>If you have flexible travel dates and a specific nonstop route in mind, the most effective approach is to set fare alerts on both Google Flights and Skyscanner simultaneously. These platforms have slightly different data sources and alert timing, so running both gives maximum coverage. When an alert fires, check the airline's own website before booking elsewhere — booking direct usually means lower fees, easier changes, and access to the airline's loyalty program points.</p>
      <p>For routes where nonstop service is seasonal, set your alert a full year before your intended travel. Airline schedules are typically released six to eleven months in advance, and the best seats — economy with extra legroom or business class awards — go quickly once schedules open.</p>
